<!-- Mapping table — this table is a `<table>`. One table is one shape, and `border-collapse` merges
     the grid lines — one row states that fact in its own content. The header color and stripes are
     not painted by hand: `<th>` takes the theme's header values and `variant="striped"` the theme's
     stripes.

     The left column carries tags verbatim in CDATA — proof that a deck about a markup can display its
     own syntax. Cells are text-only, so the CDATA is the cell's content.

     There are eight rows: text · shape · image · container · table · chart · connector · motion.
     Gathering the vocabulary on one slide makes the answer to "what does it become" read as a
     promise rather than a list. That is also why there is no caption: the table uses the full
     height, and the promise in words lives in the notes. -->
<slide id="pptx" enter="rise">
  <h1>What each tag becomes</h1>
  <table variant="striped">
    <tr><th>SOML</th><th>PPTX output</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><![CDATA[<text> · <h1> · <p> · <ul>]]></td>
      <td>p:sp text box — carries the resolved rPr</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><![CDATA[<shape> (16 presets · path)]]></td>
      <td>p:sp + prstGeom · custGeom — holds text</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><![CDATA[<image> (PNG · JPEG · SVG)]]></td>
      <td>p:pic — SVG stays vector, with a PNG fallback</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><![CDATA[<row> · <col> · <grid> · <stack>]]></td>
      <td>No shape — dissolves into children's absolute positions</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><![CDATA[<table> (this slide)]]></td>
      <td>p:graphicFrame + a:tbl — an editable table</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><![CDATA[<chart> · <series> · <point>]]></td>
      <td>Shapes + text — one bar is one shape</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><![CDATA[<connector>]]></td>
      <td>p:cxnSp — connectors glued to boxes</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><![CDATA[enter= · transition=]]></td>
      <td>p:timing · p:transition — native entrance effects</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <note>In PowerPoint you can edit the text, and click into a table cell to add a row. Drag a box and its connectors follow. This is where "a file that opens" and "a file you can edit" part ways.</note>
</slide>
